1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
Some of the biggest HSC Biology questions ask

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,440
you to assess or evaluate a biotechnology.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,280
Markers want the technology linked to its effect

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,800
on biodiversity, not just on the organism, and

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,960
ethics answers need the same link to the

6
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,720
people and animals involved.

7
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:20,560
You have the routine: a verdict, the criteria

8
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:23,440
you judge by, and evidence from the question.

9
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,440
Here are three HSC-style questions.

10
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,120
Plan each one, then we'll build the answer.

11
00:00:30,334 --> 00:00:31,213
Question one.

12
00:00:31,694 --> 00:00:34,893
A farming district switched from ordinary maize, sprayed

13
00:00:35,054 --> 00:00:37,693
against caterpillars, to a maize that makes its

14
00:00:37,774 --> 00:00:40,013
own insect-killing protein.

15
00:00:40,094 --> 00:00:42,493
The table shows data from before the switch

16
00:00:42,614 --> 00:00:44,253
and ten years later.

17
00:00:44,374 --> 00:00:47,053
Assess the effect of this change on biodiversity

18
00:00:47,094 --> 00:00:48,333
in the district.

19
00:00:48,494 --> 00:00:49,773
Pause here to answer.

20
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:03,720
Assess means judge how far, so your verdict

21
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:07,080
says how much biodiversity changed, and which way.

22
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:11,160
Your criteria are the levels: species and genetic.

23
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:13,720
The baseline is the one the question gives

24
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:16,760
you, sprayed maize, not untouched land.

25
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:18,680
Now sort the evidence.

26
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:22,040
Sprays and insect species are the species level.

27
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,760
Varieties are the crop's genetic level.

28
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:27,840
Surviving caterpillars show a genetic change in the

29
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:28,520
pest.

30
00:01:29,734 --> 00:01:32,613
Biodiversity rose at the species level, but the

31
00:01:32,694 --> 00:01:35,733
crop's genetic diversity fell, so the change is

32
00:01:35,894 --> 00:01:36,373
mixed.

33
00:01:37,734 --> 00:01:41,013
Species: sprays fell from six to one, so

34
00:01:41,174 --> 00:01:43,893
fewer insects that don't eat maize were killed,

35
00:01:43,974 --> 00:01:47,893
and recorded species rose from seventeen to twenty-five.

36
00:01:48,054 --> 00:01:52,133
Genetic: varieties fell from fifteen to four, so

37
00:01:52,294 --> 00:01:53,893
one new disease could hit most of the

38
00:01:54,214 --> 00:01:55,293
district's crop.

39
00:01:55,414 --> 00:01:58,773
And surviving caterpillars are rising: resistance alleles are

40
00:01:58,854 --> 00:02:01,213
becoming more common in the pest, which threatens

41
00:02:01,254 --> 00:02:02,373
the species gain.

42
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:06,880
Only three farms in ten plant refuges, so

43
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,720
resistance can keep spreading.

44
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,000
If it does, spraying returns, and the species

45
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:13,360
gain could be lost.

46
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,400
So close the loop.

47
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:19,520
Overall, the effect is mixed: species diversity rose

48
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:22,560
to a moderate extent, but crop genetic diversity

49
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:23,120
fell.

50
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:26,880
The gain lasts provided refuges are planted, and

51
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,200
more varieties with the trait would reduce the

52
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:30,000
loss.

53
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:33,360
Ecosystem effects aren't in the data, so don't

54
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:34,240
invent them.

55
00:02:35,467 --> 00:02:36,826
Here's a weaker answer.

56
00:02:37,467 --> 00:02:39,786
"The maize is good because it kills caterpillars

57
00:02:39,867 --> 00:02:42,906
and gives bigger harvests, but pests could become

58
00:02:42,987 --> 00:02:45,866
resistant." It's all about the organism and the

59
00:02:45,947 --> 00:02:46,746
farm.

60
00:02:46,867 --> 00:02:49,626
It never names a level of biodiversity, uses

61
00:02:49,707 --> 00:02:52,266
no data, and has no real verdict.

62
00:02:53,467 --> 00:02:54,346
Question two.

63
00:02:54,907 --> 00:02:57,706
A rare wild sheep species survives as about

64
00:02:57,787 --> 00:03:01,386
three hundred animals, all descended from eight founders.

65
00:03:02,027 --> 00:03:04,586
A cell bank holds frozen skin cells from

66
00:03:04,747 --> 00:03:07,466
ten wild sheep that died thirty years ago,

67
00:03:07,547 --> 00:03:09,866
who carried alleles now missing from the herd.

68
00:03:10,587 --> 00:03:13,226
Scientists plan to clone them, using eggs and

69
00:03:13,387 --> 00:03:15,866
surrogate mothers from domestic sheep.

70
00:03:15,947 --> 00:03:18,026
Evaluate this plan as a way to protect

71
00:03:18,067 --> 00:03:19,306
the species.

72
00:03:19,467 --> 00:03:22,826
Refer to genetic diversity, animal welfare and one

73
00:03:23,187 --> 00:03:24,106
alternative.

74
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:25,466
Pause here to answer.

75
00:03:35,867 --> 00:03:39,706
Evaluate, and the question hands you three criteria.

76
00:03:39,907 --> 00:03:44,026
First, the mechanism: each clone's nuclear DNA matches

77
00:03:44,107 --> 00:03:46,506
its cell donor, so it brings back that

78
00:03:46,627 --> 00:03:47,546
donor's alleles.

79
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:50,266
Now genetic diversity.

80
00:03:50,347 --> 00:03:53,066
Clones of ten different donors carry alleles the

81
00:03:53,147 --> 00:03:55,306
herd has lost, so when they breed with

82
00:03:55,627 --> 00:03:58,906
living sheep, those alleles re-enter the gene pool.

83
00:03:58,987 --> 00:04:01,706
But count the donors, not the clones.

84
00:04:01,947 --> 00:04:04,026
Ten clones of one sheep add no more

85
00:04:04,187 --> 00:04:05,146
alleles than one.

86
00:04:06,367 --> 00:04:07,886
Next, welfare.

87
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:10,606
If only about two in a hundred embryos

88
00:04:10,687 --> 00:04:14,046
become lambs, many domestic ewes go through embryo

89
00:04:14,127 --> 00:04:17,166
transfer, and most embryos are lost, so the

90
00:04:17,247 --> 00:04:18,806
cost to them is high.

91
00:04:19,167 --> 00:04:22,286
Then the alternative: manage breeding among the living

92
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:24,406
sheep and protect their habitat.

93
00:04:24,687 --> 00:04:27,646
That removes threats cloning can't, but it can't

94
00:04:27,767 --> 00:04:29,566
bring back lost alleles.

95
00:04:30,047 --> 00:04:31,486
So, the verdict.

96
00:04:31,567 --> 00:04:33,486
The plan is worth it to a moderate

97
00:04:33,727 --> 00:04:37,366
extent, as a small trial alongside habitat protection,

98
00:04:37,647 --> 00:04:39,806
provided the clones survive and breed into the

99
00:04:40,047 --> 00:04:40,366
herd.

100
00:04:41,567 --> 00:04:43,966
The classic mistake is writing that clones are

101
00:04:44,047 --> 00:04:48,446
identical, so cloning can never increase genetic diversity.

102
00:04:48,527 --> 00:04:51,326
A clone is identical to its donor, not

103
00:04:51,367 --> 00:04:52,606
to the herd.

104
00:04:52,887 --> 00:04:55,246
Here, the donors carry alleles the herd has

105
00:04:55,327 --> 00:04:55,966
lost.

106
00:04:57,167 --> 00:04:58,046
Question three.

107
00:04:58,367 --> 00:05:01,646
Thousands of people with kidney failure wait years

108
00:05:01,727 --> 00:05:03,006
for a donor kidney.

109
00:05:03,327 --> 00:05:06,526
Scientists have gene-edited pigs so their kidneys suit

110
00:05:06,607 --> 00:05:09,886
humans better, and inactivated copies of a virus

111
00:05:10,047 --> 00:05:11,926
built into the pig's DNA.

112
00:05:12,287 --> 00:05:15,326
Since 2024, a few patients have received these

113
00:05:15,407 --> 00:05:18,206
kidneys with special permission, and one worked for

114
00:05:18,287 --> 00:05:20,846
about nine months before it was removed.

115
00:05:21,167 --> 00:05:23,646
Assess the use of gene-edited pigs as a

116
00:05:23,807 --> 00:05:24,846
source of kidneys.

117
00:05:25,247 --> 00:05:27,566
Refer to benefits and ethical issues.

118
00:05:28,207 --> 00:05:29,566
Pause here to answer.

119
00:05:39,967 --> 00:05:43,486
Assess means judge how far, so size the

120
00:05:43,567 --> 00:05:44,806
benefit first.

121
00:05:45,087 --> 00:05:45,886
Better than what?

122
00:05:46,367 --> 00:05:48,846
Than waiting years for a human kidney, which

123
00:05:48,927 --> 00:05:50,686
some people don't survive.

124
00:05:50,767 --> 00:05:52,526
A pig could supply a kidney when it's

125
00:05:52,607 --> 00:05:53,406
needed.

126
00:05:53,567 --> 00:05:54,526
Shown how?

127
00:05:54,567 --> 00:05:57,646
A few patients, and kidneys lasting months, not

128
00:05:57,727 --> 00:05:58,686
years.

129
00:05:58,767 --> 00:06:00,526
So the benefit would be large if it

130
00:06:00,607 --> 00:06:02,766
works, but it isn't shown yet.

131
00:06:03,967 --> 00:06:06,686
Now the ethical issues, and who carries each

132
00:06:06,847 --> 00:06:07,406
one.

133
00:06:07,727 --> 00:06:10,446
The pigs are bred, confined and killed for

134
00:06:10,527 --> 00:06:11,526
their organs.

135
00:06:11,807 --> 00:06:14,206
Early patients take a high risk, so they

136
00:06:14,287 --> 00:06:16,926
need to understand it and agree freely.

137
00:06:16,967 --> 00:06:19,166
And a pig virus crossing into a patient

138
00:06:19,247 --> 00:06:21,406
could spread to other people, which is why

139
00:06:21,487 --> 00:06:23,886
those viral copies were inactivated.

140
00:06:24,007 --> 00:06:26,926
Some people object in principle, and evidence can't

141
00:06:27,007 --> 00:06:27,646
settle that.

142
00:06:28,867 --> 00:06:31,986
Gene-edited pig kidneys are justified to a moderate

143
00:06:32,067 --> 00:06:36,066
extent, as carefully regulated trials, provided patients give

144
00:06:36,187 --> 00:06:39,506
informed consent, the pigs' welfare is protected, and

145
00:06:39,587 --> 00:06:42,146
recipients are monitored for infection.

146
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:45,026
They aren't yet a replacement for human donors.

147
00:06:46,234 --> 00:06:48,793
The classic mistake is treating one kidney that

148
00:06:48,874 --> 00:06:51,193
worked for nine months as proof that pig

149
00:06:51,274 --> 00:06:53,193
kidneys solve the shortage.

150
00:06:53,234 --> 00:06:55,913
It doesn't show how long, how often, or

151
00:06:55,994 --> 00:06:56,953
how safely.

152
00:06:58,167 --> 00:07:02,006
For any biotechnology question, give one mechanism link.

153
00:07:02,487 --> 00:07:05,046
Judge it against criteria, with evidence from the

154
00:07:05,127 --> 00:07:08,726
question, naming the biodiversity level, or who carries

155
00:07:08,807 --> 00:07:09,646
each risk.

156
00:07:09,847 --> 00:07:12,006
Then finish with a verdict and a condition

157
00:07:12,087 --> 00:07:13,366
that evidence could check.

158
00:07:14,007 --> 00:07:16,966
Next, why a technology that works can still

159
00:07:17,047 --> 00:07:19,686
have little lasting effect on a population.

160
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:22,340
Here are the key notes.

161
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,500
Pause the video if you need more time

162
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:25,380
to copy them.
